{
  "slug": "vertical-order-traversal-of-a-binary-tree",
  "title": "Vertical Order Traversal of a Binary Tree",
  "topic": "trees",
  "difficulty": "Hard",
  "pattern": "tree DFS + per-column bucketing / sorting",
  "statement": "You are given a binary tree stored in a flat array `nodes` in heap layout: the root is `nodes[0]`, and for the node at index `i` its left child sits at index `2*i + 1` and its right child at index `2*i + 2`. The value `-1` marks an absent node, and real node values are never negative.\n\nGive the root the coordinate `(row, col) = (0, 0)`. Moving to a left child adds 1 to the row and subtracts 1 from the column; moving to a right child adds 1 to the row and adds 1 to the column.\n\nReport the vertical order traversal: one group per column, with the groups ordered from the smallest column to the largest. Inside one column, order the nodes by row ascending, and when two nodes share both row and column, order them by value ascending.\n\nReturn the result as a list of lists of integers. An absent root gives an empty list.\n\nConstraints: `1 <= nodes.length <= 100` and `-1 <= nodes[i] <= 100`.\n",
  "examples": [
    {
      "input": "nodes = [-1]",
      "output": "[]",
      "explanation": "Produced by the reference solution, verified against all four implementations."
    },
    {
      "input": "nodes = [30, 94, 70, 67, 32]",
      "output": "[[67], [94], [30, 32], [70]]",
      "explanation": "Produced by the reference solution, verified against all four implementations."
    }
  ],
  "constraints": [
    "Function signature: `function solve(nodes: number[])`.",
    "Inputs are JSON-serializable values satisfying the bounds stated above."
  ],
  "hints": [
    "Give every node two numbers while you walk: a column, which decreases by one on a left move and increases by one on a right move, and a row, which is simply the depth.",
    "Traverse the tree once and store a triple (column, row, value) per node instead of writing straight into the answer, because later nodes can belong to columns you already emitted.",
    "Order the triples by column, then by row, then by value, and split the sorted list into a new group each time the column changes."
  ],
  "editorial": {
    "approach": "The brute force walks the tree once with an explicit stack, recording a triple (column, row, value) for every reachable node, then orders those triples with a selectionsort-style pass that repeatedly scans the unsorted tail for the smallest triple, and finally groups equal columns while scanning the ordered list. The optimal version performs the same single walk but buckets each node into a hash map keyed by column, so only the column keys get sorted; the (row, value) pairs inside a bucket are then sorted with a comparison sort.",
    "why_optimal": "Every node must be visited at least once, and within a column the output is ordered by (row, value), so the values of a column must be compared with each other. When a single column can hold up to n nodes, sorting that column costs O(n log n), which is the real lower bound here, so the O(n log n) bound is tight for this task. The brute force pays O(n^2) comparisons because it sorts the entire triple set by scanning for a minimum instead of using the structure of the columns. With n <= 100 both fit comfortably, but the difference is visible once a column collects many nodes.",
    "pitfalls": "Using children 2*i and 2*i+1 instead of 2*i+1 and 2*i+2, which silently shifts every column. Reading nodes[i] before checking nodes[i] == -1, so absent slots leak into the answer. Breaking ties by insertion order instead of by value, which breaks the required total order between nodes that share a row and a column. Assuming a perfect tree: children indices often fall past the end of the array, and the children of an absent node must be ignored even when they hold a value."
  },
